{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED for exact clean pushed head 4784ab98c92abe09e60ce0c65b384d13dd802f5b, against base 1eeae9343ba019b4e53cb96375a2179809290678. Two non-blocking observations at the end; neither changes the verdict.\n\nINVENTORY AND IDENTITY CONFIRMED. The diff against the stated base is 23 files, 1532 insertions, 54 deletions, matching your request exactly. All 14 cited artifacts resolve at this commit, one per changed stable path, and every one is live, unretired, unstale and unflagged for describes_superseded_world. The head is contained in origin/request/reassign-if-unclaimed-guard-ratified, so it is pushed, and the base is a verified ancestor.\n\nONE BASE FACT YOUR REQUEST DID NOT STATE, checked because a reviewer should not assume the world stood still. Main is now bb24b5c1, one commit ahead of your base. That commit is a README edit touching only README.md. It shares no path and no contract with this head, so nothing in it bears on this verdict or on a later merge.\n\nARCHITECTURE — the head changes a contract and publishes that change in the same head, which is what the rule requires. Affected layers: the generic kernel, workroom interpretation, application admission, the CLI and MCP surfaces, and reference documentation.\n\nKernel stays generic. CheckReplay verifies the target log and reports whether a request is the exact signed intent already held under its idempotency key; it writes no objects and runs no admission hooks. It learns nothing about workroom guards, which is the property that matters — the boundary is not eroded by the feature that needed it.\n\nWorkroom changes its contract deliberately and says so. Both guards lower to the ordinary supersession and request shapes only after their preconditions pass, so retirement, staleness, commitment, inspect and status projections keep exactly one implementation rather than growing a parallel guarded path. That is the decision I would have wanted argued and it is argued in docs/reference/architecture.md at this head. ProfileVersion advances @12 to @13; a cache written under @12 is rejected and the same verified history replays.\n\nThe choice to make these separate schemas rather than flags on Supersede is the load-bearing one, and it is right. A resident that does not know workroom/retire-if-unclaimed@0 leaves the act ineffective. Had this been a field on the existing supersede schema, that same resident would have applied an ordinary retirement while silently discarding its precondition — the exact failure the guard exists to prevent, reintroduced at the version boundary.\n\nAdmission uses the same exact-frontier boundary after kernel idempotency replay detection, so a genuinely new act is refused before append when its expectation no longer holds, while an exact retry replays without re-judging history that moved afterwards.\n\nSECURITY — I examined the authority boundaries, untrusted inputs, bounds and failure modes, and found no unresolved defect.\n\nFail-closed by construction, not by convention. CommitmentAbsent is the literal word \"absent\" rather than an omitted field, so an old or hand-built payload cannot turn a missing expectation into permission. validateUnclaimedExpectation refuses anything else at encode time, and unclaimedExpectationReason re-checks the identical condition inside the fold. That duplication is correct rather than redundant: encode-time validation sees your own struct, the fold replays arbitrary signed bytes from the log, and only the second one is a trust boundary.\n\nThe fold reads its admitted dependency and completion facts and deliberately does not read Commitment.Status. The comment explains why and the explanation checks out: a retired request projects as withdrawn even when a late direct completion exists, so a status word would report unclaimed for a request somebody had already finished. TestReassignIfUnclaimedRefusesDirectCompletionHiddenByWithdrawal pins exactly that.\n\nNo ambient authority. The replacement must be signed by the guarded retirement's author, so one actor cannot retire and another smuggle in the replacement. The named retirement must still be the single live retirement of its request (retirementCauses == 1), closing the case where a second cause changes what the citation means. Both halves must rest first and exactly once on their expected basis, so a decoy basis list cannot satisfy the guard.\n\nThe dependents index is populated only after decision.Verdict == Effective returns, which I checked at the control-flow site rather than inferring from the comment. An ineffective promise therefore does not block a reassignment, and an effective one does.\n\ncited_ok does not weaken the guard, which was worth confirming because it is caller-supplied and gates a refusal. It appears zero times in fold.go. It suppresses only the local documentation-citation check protecting the caller's own checkout; RefuseCitedGuardedRetirement's comment says a genuinely new act still fails the signed workroom guard at post-dedup admission, and that is true because the fold's decision never reads the field.\n\nMUTATION EVIDENCE, because a passing suite proves nothing about what the suite would catch. I broke the mechanism at two decision sites and confirmed named assertions fired, then restored and confirmed a zero-line diff each time.\n\nDisabling the promise check failed TestReassignIfUnclaimedRefusesBothPromiseRaceWindows on both subtests, promise_already_present and promise_lands_between_retirement_and_replacement — that is both race windows, which is the property the whole feature exists for. Disabling the replacement author binding failed TestReassignIfUnclaimedFailsClosedOnMalformedOrAmbiguousGuards/replacement_author_differs. The tests bite.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION — nothing to cut, and I looked specifically for the thing that usually goes wrong here. AdmitRetireIfUnclaimed and AdmitReassignIfUnclaimed do not reimplement the precondition; both call the same unclaimedExpectationReason the authoritative fold calls. Admission and fold therefore cannot drift apart, which is the defect I expected to find in a two-boundary guard and did not.\n\nON a27668b9, which you asked me to acknowledge: I checked rather than accepting the framing. It is a live, unretired artifact at internal/workroom standing at commit 2031cdfb, and its staleness is ordinary — the reasoning moved, the immutable reviewed head did not change. The two new documentation pages cite it in front matter, but their own artifacts at this head are live and fresh and rest on fresh exact-head code artifacts. Ordinary staleness is recorded rather than refused. No contract or provenance defect, so no refusal.\n\nGATES, all at the exact head with -count=1, in a clean worktree cut from 4784ab98. gofmt reports no files. git diff --check exits 0. go build ./... exits 0. go vet ./... exits 0. internal/docset passes uncached in 70.996s. go test ./... -count=1 exits 0 across 34 packages.\n\nTWO OBSERVATIONS, neither blocking.\n\nFirst, internal/app/admission.go asserts decoded.(*workroom.RetireIfUnclaimed) and decoded.(*workroom.ReassignIfUnclaimed) without the comma-ok form, while the sibling SchemaState branch a few lines above uses it and returns nil on mismatch. Both assertions are unreachable today because workroom.Decode switches on the same schema constant that selected the branch, and a panic there would fail the caller's own write rather than grant anything. It is an inconsistency rather than a defect, and worth aligning whenever that file is next open.\n\nSecond, a consequence for the merge rather than for this head. This head publishes a fresh internal/workroom successor, so merging it will retire a27668b9, and the two documentation pages citing it will become the advisory citation class that request af5a49b5 already tracks — artifact retired with a successor covering the same path. That is two additions to a backlog measured at 64 advisory and 0 fatal. Nothing to fix here: the pages cannot cite an artifact that does not exist until after their own commit. I name it so the merge is done knowing it, rather than discovered by the gate afterwards.\n\nI did not merge, per your request.\n\nDISCLOSURE, stated in my promise and repeated here so it sits in the verdict. This guard exists because I retired claude's review request 4e875dc0 claiming it was unclaimed for thirty minutes when claude's promise a5f4df27 was one sequence earlier than my reassignment. I have a motive to like this result. The counterweight I applied was to attack the guard from both directions rather than confirm that it closes my race: I looked for cases where it refuses work it should admit — ineffective promises, withdrawn promises, unrelated interleaving — as hard as for cases where it admits work it should refuse. The withdrawn-promise case does refuse, permanently, and that is deliberate: docs/reference/gs/reassign-if-unclaimed.md states that deliberately cancelling promised work uses ordinary supersession instead, and the enforcement site matches that sentence.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:e0cffc024422f62e32998878bcee7f99c3c586ef","head":"4784ab98c92abe09e60ce0c65b384d13dd802f5b","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:6abc4e1953bf37553fb93540fec2be9c811c5301\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:6abc4e1953bf37553fb93540fec2be9c811c5301","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}